    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIODEBUGSEL  */ "This register selects debug signals from chipcommon on to GPIOs.",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOEVENT     */ "The 4-byte General Purpose I/O Event register is writable and has a reset value of zero.\nBit N of this register is set when an edge is detected on the GPIO[N] input of the device.\nHardware detects either rising or falling edges depending on the value of bit N of the GPIOEventIntPolarity register.\nBits is this register are cleared only by software writing them to one; writing 0 to bits in this register has no effect.",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOEVENTINTMASK */ "The 4-byte General Purpose I/O Event Mask register is writable and has a reset value of zero. Bit N of this register is set to enable GPIO event interrupts. The contents of the GPIOEvent register are ANDed with the contents of this register. A result of 1 for any GPIO input causes a GPIO interrupt. Bits in this register should be set to enable event interrupts on the corresponding input signal and cleared to disable them.",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOEVENTINTPOLARITY */ "The 4-byte General Purpose I/O Event Polarity register is writable and has a reset value of zero. Bit N of this register is set to detect falling edge transitions of the GPIO(N) input and cleared to detect rising edge transitions of that input.",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOINPUT     */ "The 4-byte General Purpose I/O Input register is read-only and always contains the values that are present on the GPIO pins, including those being driven by the device.",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOINTMASK   */ "The 4-byte General Purpose I/O Input Interrupt Mask register is writable, has a reset value of zero, and determines which GPIO inputs are being used as interrupt pins. The GPIO input signals are XORed with the contents of the GPIOIntPolarity register and then ANDed with the contents of this register. A result of 1 for any GPIO input generates a GPIO interrupt. Bits in this register should be set to 1 to enable interrupts on the corresponding input signal and should be cleared to disable them.",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOINTPOLARITY */ "The 4-byte General Purpose I /O Input Interrupt Polarity register is writable, has a reset value of zero, and determines the polarity of GPIO inputs used as interrupt pins. Each bit in this register is XORed with the corresponding GPIO input signal and then ANDed with the corresponding bit of the GPIOIntMask? register. If the result is 1 for any GPIO input, a GPIO interrupt is raised. Bits in this register should be set if an interrupt should be asserted when the input signal is low and should b ...",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOOUT       */ "The 4-byte General Purpose I/O Output register is writable, has a reset value of zero, and contains an arbitrary value that is placed on the GPIO pins. Each pin is driven only when the corresponding bit in the GPIOOutEn register is set. When read, this register returns the value last written.",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOOUTEN     */ "The 4-byte General Purpose I/O Output Enable register is writable, has a reset value of zero, and determines which GPIO pins are used as inputs and which are used as outputs. When a bit in this register is set, the corresponding GPIO pin is driven as an output.",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOTIMEROUTMASK */ "The 4-byte GPIO Timer Output Mask register is writable and contains the value 0x0 at device reset. When bit x of this register contains 1, hardware masks the output enable for GPIO x with the GPIO timer output enable described in the documentation for the GPIOTimerVal register. When bit x contains zero, the GPIO timer does not affect the corresponding GPIO.\nThis register affects only those GPIOs being driven by the chipcommon core (where the corresponding GPIOControl bit is 0).",
    /* CHIPCOMMONA_GPIOTIMERVAL  */ "The four-byte GPIO timer value register is writable and contains the value 0x0 at device reset. This register contains 16-bit timer ON and timer OFF values that control enables for timer-driven GPIOs. Use of this feature reduces power consumption on GPIOs used to drive LEDs, where precise on and off times are not required. A hardware counter is alternately reloaded with OnCount and OffCount when it decrements to zero. The core sets a timer output enable signal when it loads the counter with OnCo ...",
